00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.750
I'm focused on
working with users

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.638
who are trying to run
Kubernetes on GKE in production.

00:00:37.638 --> 00:00:38.430
KASLIN FIELDS: Wow.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.380
So you must have some
really cool insights

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.730
into how this stuff
works in the real world,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.130
so I'm really
excited to see what

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.422
you're going to teach us today.

00:00:45.422 --> 00:00:48.088
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Definitely just
trying to build on the concepts

00:00:48.088 --> 00:00:50.430
that you and Carter have
introduced in previous episodes

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.750
of "Kubernetes Essentials."

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.610
Specifically, we're
going to take a look

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.880
at how you can work with
namespaces and services

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.980
when something is going
wrong with the deployment

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.570
of your application
in Kubernetes.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.400
They each have
respective properties

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.620
that we can take a look
at to help us debug

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.200
and help us remediate
whatever issue we're

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.037
running into in our
Kubernetes environment.

00:01:09.037 --> 00:01:09.870
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.480
So what are we going
to start out with?

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.980
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
Well, for starters,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.280
we're going to have a
sample application running

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.600
in a Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.180
And that sample
application is comprised

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.590
of a frontend pod, which is
fronted by a service-type load

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.260
balancer, so we have a public
IP address so we can access it

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.130
in a browser.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.020
And that frontend
pod is communicating

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.280
with the backend pod.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.440
And so the backend pod is
returning a whole bunch

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.680
of information and metadata
about the environment

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:37.740
in which it's running.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.950
And we can actually see
here the specific requests

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.335
that the frontend is
issuing to the backend.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.210
If we take a look at
the Kubernetes manifest,

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.350
we'll see here that they're
both represented by deployments,

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:52.320
and they each reside within
their own specific namespaces.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.980
So in this specific scenario,
we have the frontend

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.610
that is managed by Team A,
running in namespace team-a.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.080
And then we have the
backend application,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.937
which is owned by Team B,
running in namespace team-b.

00:02:03.937 --> 00:02:04.770
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.199
So here we have a website which
has a frontend and a backend,

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:11.870
and the frontend and
the backend are each

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:13.440
owned by separate teams.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.510
And those teams are utilizing
different namespaces.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.603
So what are we going
to look into next?

00:02:19.603 --> 00:02:21.020
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
Well, we're going

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.510
to take a look at
the namespaces a bit

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.060
further so that we can
understand what constraints

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.460
could actually be defined
at the namespace level that

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.530
might actually affect the
deployments of our pods

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.580
and applications.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.610
So as you can see,
namespaces are a great way

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.590
to divide the resources
within a given

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.540
cluster across various teams.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.730
And namespaces give
you a lot of control

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:46.890
between what you can actually
access within a given cluster.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.820
So as you can see here,
we have the frontend

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.330
is owned by Team A, and we
have a namespace called team-a.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:57.020
And then we have the backend,
which is owned by Team B,

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.837
and it's in the
namespace team-b.

00:02:58.837 --> 00:02:59.670
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.903
So let's take a deeper
look at these namespaces.

00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:04.070
ANTHONY BUSHONG: So when
digging into the namespaces

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:05.570
within a given
cluster, it can often

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.195
be a bit difficult to
navigate and understand

00:03:09.195 --> 00:03:10.820
which namespace you're
working against.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.470
So the kubens tool
is a great tool

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.390
to show all the namespaces
in your cluster,

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:17.510
as well as which one your
Kubernetes context is currently

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.620
configured to interact with.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.620
Once we know what namespace
we're working with--

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:22.890
team-b in this case--

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.090
we can run commands
like kubectl auth can-i

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.330
and define the
verbs and resources

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:29.580
that we want to interact with.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.610
So if we want to
delete or create,

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.950
let's say, pods, we want to
be able to check and ensure

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.350
that we can actually
interact with these resources

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.030
in that given namespace.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.952
And so once that
we have verified

00:03:42.952 --> 00:03:44.660
that we have permissions
to the namespace

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.680
to interact with the
resources we need to,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.355
we can actually
describe the namespace

00:03:48.355 --> 00:03:50.480
and take a look at some of
the constraints that are

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.440
defined at a namespace level.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.050
The first one is
resource quotas.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:57.170
So it's a great way
to limit the resources

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.730
that a team can use within
a given Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:03.350
So in this case, we're
defining a pod resource quota,

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.030
and it's a great
use case for trying

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.690
to preserve IP allocation
across multiple teams sharing

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.290
the same Kubernetes cluster.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.950
So in this case, we're ensuring
that this team can only

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.850
run five pods, which means
five IPs that it will consume.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:18.769
We also want to define
resource limits.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:22.190
Basically, these are
just limit ranges

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:26.000
that define our minimum
and maximum amount of CPU

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.210
that containers can request
when running in this namespace.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:31.940
And it's a great
place to troubleshoot,

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:33.607
because either of
these could be reasons

00:04:33.607 --> 00:04:35.780
why your deployment fails
or why your deployment

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.600
is being blocked because
there are constraints in place

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:39.890
within that given namespace.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:41.390
KASLIN FIELDS: So
namespaces give us

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:45.740
a way to manage and isolate
resources on some basis--

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.660
in this case, on a team basis.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.270
And there are also
controls in there

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.240
that we can use to
make sure that all

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.520
of the resources for
that team are running

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.080
the way that we expect them to.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.122
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Exactly.

00:04:58.122 --> 00:04:58.870
Exactly.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:00.620
Like limit ranges,
resource quotas-- these

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.960
are all things that could be
defined for various teams.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.090
Role-based access control
is a really good way

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:08.630
to not just isolate
individual user access

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:11.090
to various resources,
but also service accounts

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.010
that workloads use
if they require

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:16.560
different levels of access
within the cluster, as well.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.975
And this is just the
tip of the iceberg.

00:05:18.975 --> 00:05:21.350
There are many things that
are namespace-scoped-- network

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:22.730
policies, for example.

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:24.563
That can be, again,
different constraints

00:05:24.563 --> 00:05:26.480
that are defined for
where your workloads run.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:27.313
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:05:27.313 --> 00:05:29.190
So what are we going
to dive into next?

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.857
ANTHONY BUSHONG: So
we took a little bit

00:05:30.857 --> 00:05:32.300
about what we can
do to understand

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.670
how namespaces may be
affecting our interactions

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:35.353
with Kubernetes.

00:05:35.353 --> 00:05:37.520
So the next thing that we're
going to take a look at

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.310
are Kubernetes services and
how we can interact with them

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.890
to understand what may be going
wrong with our application

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.527
running in a Kubernetes cluster.

00:05:44.527 --> 00:05:45.360
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:46.967
So let's take a
deeper look at them.

00:05:46.967 --> 00:05:48.050
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Happy to.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:51.860
So if we take a look at the
frontend that we deployed,

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.905
we can actually see the
host that the frontend--

00:05:54.905 --> 00:05:56.780
or the hostname that
the frontend is actually

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.930
issuing a request against, which
is the backend service that

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:01.950
is owned by Team B.

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.740
And so Kubernetes
services, as you've

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.140
talked about in
the past, Kaslin,

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:11.460
are a nice abstraction on top
of Kubernetes pods or replicas

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:12.450
of a given pod.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.023
And so rather than trying to
think about a per-pod level,

00:06:15.023 --> 00:06:16.440
we can think about
a service level

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.762
that maps to numerous
replicas of a pod.

00:06:18.762 --> 00:06:20.220
And it's represented
by the objects

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.710
you see here-- pods,
services, and endpoints.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.960
And so when we want to
troubleshoot something locally,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.290
we can actually port
forward against the service

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.890
where we actually are,
again, port forwarding

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:36.870
interactions with our backend
to our local 8080 port.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.030
So as you can see, I'm
going to issue a command

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:43.580
to localhost on port
8080, and we're actually

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:46.010
going to get a response
directly from our backend that

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.900
is running in the
team-b namespace.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.290
So basically, when you're going
through the debugging cycle,

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.330
if you need to interact
with services locally

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:57.500
or things that are a little
harder to visualize with a web

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.450
UI or something like
that, you can actually

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:01.640
port forward it to your
development workstation

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:04.333
and begin to interact
with it on your localhost.

00:07:04.333 --> 00:07:05.750
KASLIN FIELDS: So
services give us

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:09.440
a way to interact with
workloads running on Kubernetes.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.720
We can run our apps in pods.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.850
But if you have multiple
copies of those pods,

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:17.030
then how do you interact
with the workload as a whole?

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:18.958
And services give us that.

00:07:18.958 --> 00:07:20.000
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Exactly.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.040
And you get a DNS record
that gets provisioned.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.670
So again, you don't even have
to remember that virtual IP

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.110
or that cluster IP address.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.620
You can actually
natively interact with it

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.300
with a DNS hostname
that gets-- or a DNS

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:36.050
record that gets provisioned to
kube-dns or your cluster's DNS.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:39.470
And so it's really useful for,
again, clients within a cluster

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.180
to interact with
your application.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.435
But it's, again, also
useful for end users

00:07:43.435 --> 00:07:44.810
trying to understand
what's going

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:48.477
on with the port forward
command with kubectl.

00:07:48.477 --> 00:07:49.310
KASLIN FIELDS: Nice.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:51.630
So where do we go from here?

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:54.140
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Well, we
showed a little bit about DNS.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:58.190
See, now there's the age old
joke about how it's always DNS,

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:00.740
so I think it's important to
understand a bit more about how

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.200
we can interact and
troubleshoot with the DNS

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.532
in our Kubernetes cluster.

00:08:04.532 --> 00:08:06.740
KASLIN FIELDS: All right,
so let's take a look at it.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:10.087
What have you got to show
us when we're debugging DNS?

00:08:10.087 --> 00:08:12.170
ANTHONY BUSHONG: So I think
the first thing that's

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:15.020
important to capture
and understand

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.450
is where kube-dns is actually
running within our cluster.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.570
So we're going to
run kubens again,

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:24.300
and we're actually going to see
a namespace called kube-system.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:27.110
And so kube-system is
where system pods that

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:30.590
are required for the cluster to
function, like DNS, typically

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:31.760
reside.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:35.835
So if we actually run kubectl
get pods in grep for kube-dns,

00:08:35.835 --> 00:08:37.460
we're going to see
a few pods running--

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.601
two replicas of kube-dns,
as well as an autoscaler.

00:08:40.601 --> 00:08:42.559
So I think it's really
important to understand,

00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:47.240
as your cluster scales up,
as your workloads scale up,

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.745
the number of DNS queries
also typically goes up.

00:08:50.745 --> 00:08:52.370
And so typically,
you want to make sure

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:56.450
that your kube-dns pods are
actually scaling up properly

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:02.540
and are able to handle all
of the various volume of DNS

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.868
queries coming
from your services.

00:09:04.868 --> 00:09:06.410
And so when something
is going wrong,

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:07.785
if you take a look
at this, we've

00:09:07.785 --> 00:09:11.910
navigated to a YAML file
for a dnsutils container.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:16.130
And this is actually giving us
a way to interact with kube-dns

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:18.565
without having to
package up tools

00:09:18.565 --> 00:09:19.940
that we would use
to troubleshoot

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.040
into our application containers.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:25.590
So once we actually apply
the dnsutils.yaml file,

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.800
you'll see that this
single pod called

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.600
dnsutils has been created.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.630
And then we're going
to take and just verify

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:34.010
what namespace it's in.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.430
And so we're going to
switch to cluster-utils--

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:41.300
again, a great way to
separate tooling we

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.560
use to troubleshoot from
application workloads.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.460
Then we're going to run
the kubectl exec command.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:50.060
And we're actually going to pass
in a couple flags, -i and -t.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:51.800
And this is basically
a way for us

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:56.000
to kick off an interactive
shell within the dnsutils pod.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.340
And the dnsutils pod
will have tooling

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.740
that we may want to use
to troubleshoot DNS,

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.180
like nslookup.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.700
And so it's also
important to know

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:08.360
that, while you could get the
interactive shell by passing

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:12.860
-i and -t, you can also just
execute the single command

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:17.180
if that's what the
use case calls for,

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.640
and not an interactive shell.

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:20.390
So what we're doing
here is we're actually

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:23.190
testing the DNS resolution
for our backend service.

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:27.260
So we're using
gceme-backend.team-b,

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:30.230
and we'll see that we actually
got the cluster IP returned

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:30.935
to us properly.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:38.560
And so once we actually
run kubectl get svc,

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.070
we'll actually see that
this is the same cluster

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:44.967
IP that was returned
from the DNS resolution.

00:10:44.967 --> 00:10:45.800
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:48.170
So what you did here
is really interesting.

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:51.590
You created a new
pod within Kubernetes

00:10:51.590 --> 00:10:54.740
that had these dnsutils
on it, and then you

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.930
used that pod to debug
what was going on

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:59.120
with DNS in the cluster.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.730
That's really
interesting and gives you

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:03.770
a really nice way
to debug what's

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:06.200
happening with DNS
without even messing

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.840
with your other existing
applications in the cluster.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:09.757
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Yeah.

00:11:09.757 --> 00:11:10.430
Exactly, right?

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:13.250
I think it gives you a safe way
to package up a set of tools

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:16.012
that you only need to use for
a specific period of time,

00:11:16.012 --> 00:11:17.720
and then you can spin
it down once you're

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:21.350
done troubleshooting or you've
identified the root cause.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.230
And I think this really helps
minimize what we actually

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:26.510
need to bake into our
application containers.

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:29.220
And that's useful for a
whole bunch of reasons,

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.220
whether it's security,
whether it's performance.

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:33.590
There are a bunch of
different angles and benefits

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:36.290
that we get from making sure
that we're not basically

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:40.140
causing bloat within our
application containers.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:42.650
KASLIN FIELDS: So what else
do we want to go over today?

00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:44.025
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
Well, I think it

00:11:44.025 --> 00:11:49.430
was really cool to see the exec
functionality within kubectl

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:53.350
for our toolbox or
dnsutils container.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.405
But I also want to show
that this is something

00:11:55.405 --> 00:11:57.530
that you could do with your
application containers,

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:58.170
as well.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.200
So we'll take a little
bit further look

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:03.620
as to when we would running
an exec into our application

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:04.820
containers.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:07.850
So kubectl exec--
great for utilizing

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:09.950
with utility or
debugging containers,

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:12.710
but also can be used with
your application containers,

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:13.753
as well.

00:12:13.753 --> 00:12:15.170
This is useful if
you need to take

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:18.410
a look at the environment
in which your application is

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:18.910
running.

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:20.493
KASLIN FIELDS: So
if something's going

00:12:20.493 --> 00:12:22.970
wrong with your application
as it's running in Kubernetes,

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:25.010
we can use this tool
to dive into them

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:26.570
and figure out what's happening.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:27.620
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Exactly.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:29.960
So just building
upon the example

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:34.400
that we have in this
video today here,

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:35.960
we're going to take a look at--

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:38.150
we're going to switch back
to the team-b namespace,

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:39.775
and we're actually
going to take a look

00:12:39.775 --> 00:12:41.750
at our backend container
or our pod here that's

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.470
running serving our backend.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:47.360
And we're actually going to run
the same kubectl exec command,

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:50.190
and we're going to
provide the pod name.

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:53.300
So in this case, the
backend production

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:57.120
pod with a specific
hash at the end.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.480
And then we're going to kick
off an interactive shell

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:00.440
once again.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:03.110
And so this is a way
for us to understand

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.520
what's going on in our
actual runtime environment.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.495
So I just ran print
env, and I can actually

00:13:07.495 --> 00:13:09.620
see all of the different
environment variables that

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:12.390
are configured within this
environment, this container.

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:14.240
And so it's really
useful if you need

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.820
to take a peek at things that
might change or want to verify

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.220
that things are correct
within the actual container

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:20.172
that you're running.

00:13:20.172 --> 00:13:22.130
And another thing that's
interesting about this

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:25.610
is that, going back to, again,
the conversation of creating

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:28.400
minimal containers, sometimes,
you actually won't even

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.950
have a running shell
or something that you

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:33.500
could initiate a connection to.

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.410
And so in those
scenarios, kubectl

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:39.110
has actually added functionality
for mounting a debugging

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:42.080
container into that running pod.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:43.940
So if you don't have
a running shell,

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:46.293
there's actually relatively
new functionality

00:13:46.293 --> 00:13:48.710
that enables you to still get
that interactive environment

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:51.043
where you can do things like
check environment variables

00:13:51.043 --> 00:13:53.720
or other things that might
affect your application

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:55.355
while you're troubleshooting.

00:13:55.355 --> 00:13:57.230
KASLIN FIELDS: Thanks
for that demo, Anthony.

00:13:57.230 --> 00:13:59.360
You covered a lot of
really cool stuff in there

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.012
and a lot of tips and tricks
that even I didn't know.

00:14:02.012 --> 00:14:03.470
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
And I really think

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.780
we're just scratching the tip
of the iceberg here, right?

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:08.120
I think I just really wanted
to take a look at namespaces,

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:09.787
and services, and
some of the essentials

00:14:09.787 --> 00:14:12.560
that are important that folks
interacting with Kubernetes

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:15.080
should be aware of when trying
to figure out when things are

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:17.420
going wrong, why
they are going wrong,

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:19.230
trying to remediate them.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:22.190
But, of course, there's so
much more to dive into, right?

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:24.980
KASLIN FIELDS: Today we learned
about how Kubernetes namespaces

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.860
can help you isolate and
manage resources-- for example,

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:29.390
on a per-team basis.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:32.630
And we learned how services can
help you reach your workloads

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:34.310
and can help you debug them.

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:36.440
Then we learned some
great tips and tricks

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:39.560
for debugging both networking
problems from kube-dns

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:41.390
and how to get into
your applications

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:43.820
to debug them using kube exec.

